      "text": "\n\n<think>\n\n</think>\n\nThis is a classic performance engineering scenario often referred to as the difference between **warm throughput** (or cached throughput) and **cold throughput** (or fresh inference throughput).\n\nHere is a detailed analysis of the causes, methods to isolate valid metrics, and the necessary data points for publishing a credible benchmark.\n\n### 1. Plausible Causes for the Discrepancy\n\nThe drop in tokens per second (TPS) on unique prompts is almost certainly due to the **Prefill Phase** dominating the latency, whereas repeated prompts benefit from **KV Cache Reuse**.\n\n#### A. The Prefill vs. Decode Distinction\nLLM inference has two distinct phases:\n1.  **Prefill (Prompt Processing):** The model processes the entire input prompt in parallel to generate the initial Key-Value (KV) cache. This is compute-bound and scales with prompt length.\n2.  **Decode (Token Generation):** The model generates tokens one by one. This is memory-bandwidth-bound and scales with batch size and model size.\n\n*   **Repeated Prompts:** If the system supports **Prefix Caching** (e.g., vLLM, TGI, or SGLang), the KV cache for the repeated prompt is already in GPU memory. The \"Prefill\" phase is skipped or drastically reduced. The server jumps straight to \"Decode,\" which is much faster per token.\n*   **Unique Prompts:** Every unique prompt requires a full Prefill. If the prompt is long, this phase takes significant time. If you measure TPS as `(Total Tokens Generated) / (Total Time)`, the long Prefill time inflates the denominator, lowering the apparent TPS.\n\n#### B. Batch Size and Scheduling Effects\n*   **Repeated Prompts:** Often, benchmarks with repeated prompts are run in a tight loop. If the server batches requests, repeated prompts may be processed in larger, more efficient batches (higher GPU utilization).\n*   **Unique Prompts:** If unique prompts are sent sequentially or with variable lengths, the scheduler may not form optimal batches, leading to lower GPU utilization and higher per-token latency.\n\n#### C. Memory Pressure and Eviction\n*   **Repeated Prompts:** The KV cache for the prompt stays in GPU HBM (High Bandwidth Memory).\n*   **Unique Prompts:** Each new prompt requires allocating new KV cache space. If the GPU memory is near capacity, the system may need to:\n    *   Evict",
